# Tooling

[P2] defaults. Existing manifests, lockfiles, tool configs, and CI scripts win.

## Package Managers

- Use the repo's existing package manager.
- New Python projects in this family: prefer `uv`.
- New Node tooling: prefer local package scripts and locked deps over global installs or unpinned `npx`.
- Before adding deps, inspect manifest and lockfile; justify why existing deps are insufficient.

## Checks

- Prefer repo-defined scripts: `make check`, `npm test`, `cargo test`, `go test`, `uv run`, or CI-equivalent.
- Run narrow checks first; run broad checks before final when the change touches shared behavior, public APIs, security, data, or build tooling.
- If a tool is missing, report `not available`; never pretend it passed.

## Formatting and linters (single source of truth)

- The repo's own config is authoritative for style: `pyproject.toml`/`ruff.toml`, `.editorconfig`, `.golangci.yml`, `rustfmt.toml`, `eslint.config.*`, `.shellcheckrc`, or the stack's equivalent. Editor, pre-commit, and CI all read these same files — never a personal editor default. Keep each setting (e.g. line length) in ONE place.
- Make pre-commit the enforcement spine: `pre-commit install` so the formatters/linters that gate CI also run on every commit against the staged snapshot. Never `--no-verify`.
- Keep format-on-save ON only where the repository gate defines the formatter and config (e.g. ruff reading the repo's `pyproject.toml`, shfmt reading `.editorconfig`). Point each language's default formatter at its gate tool reading the repo config; never run a second formatter or generic "organize imports" alongside it — they fight and churn. If a file type has no gated formatter, disable format-on-save for that file type until the project deliberately selects and gates one.
- Editor auto-format resolves config from the window's root: open each repo as its own root (or a multi-root workspace) so the tool finds THAT repo's config, not a parent or built-in default. Symptom of a bad root: editor wraps at 88 while the repo pins 100, or strips an import blank line the linter keeps.
- Pin the SAME tool version across the editor extension, the pre-commit rev, and CI so none disagree.
- Never lint or reformat generated code or test fixtures — `extend-exclude` them; modernizing an intentionally-old fixture defeats its purpose.
- If editor output differs from the gate, the editor is misconfigured — fix the config once; don't hand-fix the same file repeatedly. Treat recurring formatter drift as a config bug, not a chore.

## Git

- Commit atomically with imperative messages.
- A commit/publish task packages already-accepted work; it is not authorization for a review-fix loop. Limit commit-time edits to mechanical staging, formatter, hook, metadata, signing, or publication blockers.
- Report behavioral defects, design issues, and missing tests directly in chat, then continue the requested commit/publication flow. Do not patch them, defer an otherwise authorized commit because of them, or create a findings file unless explicitly requested.
- If a configured hook physically blocks the commit because of a product defect, report the exact blocker without bypassing the hook or silently switching to development work. Product remediation requires a separate explicit fix request.
- Complete the Git workflow implied by the task, including push, PR creation, branch cleanup, or history repair when needed. Verify the remote, branch, and content before publication.
- Preserve protected/default-branch policy unless the task or established repository workflow selects that branch.

## Databases and Environments

- Resolve the real environment and target before migrations or data mutation. Use the narrowest operation and capture a rollback point when the system supports one.
- Prefer local, disposable, or staging environments for proof.
